    question: 'At a continuing-task time-limit cutoff, what should happen to the value bootstrap and the reverse GAE trace?'
    options:
      0: 'Zero both because the environment reset'
      1: 'Bootstrap from the final pre-reset observation, then stop the trace'
      2: 'Bootstrap from the reset observation and continue the trace'
      3: 'Continue the trace but suppress the bootstrap'
    answer: 1
    explanation: 'A collector cutoff does not end the continuing task, so its final pre-reset state supplies the value bootstrap. The trace stops at the cutoff to prevent advantages from a later reset episode from crossing the boundary.'
    diagnosis: 'If the timeout target changes when the reset state changes, inspect whether the collector overwrote `next_observation`. If advantages cross episode boundaries, inspect the trace mask separately from the bootstrap mask. If the fitted error is flat, verify active rows and target gradients; padding should neither train the table nor count in the denominator.'
    takeaways:
      0: 'Fit value predictions on live transitions to sampled discounted returns using the same gamma as GAE, and keep targets fixed during each critic update.'
      1: 'Use the actual final observation for an external cutoff bootstrap, then stop the reverse trace at the reset boundary.'
      2: 'A true terminal suppresses bootstrap; a true finite-horizon deadline also has no future objective beyond the task horizon.'
    visual:
      title: 'Value error falls while the table learns sampled returns'
      kind: 'executed'
      prediction: 'Will the error reach zero, and does a decreasing critic loss prove that the policy improved?'
      reading: 'The line plots masked mean-squared error after each gradient update on 512 episodes collected with seed 313. With gamma=0.9 discounted targets, it falls from about 0.3258 to 0.1359. The remaining error is expected because the four-entry position table omits remaining time and sampled returns vary. This plot measures critic fit only.'
      connection: "Every plotted point comes from `fit_value_function` on retained rollout data. The task's existing policy objectives and their exact-return evaluation remain separate; the critic extension has no continuous-control or actor-improvement claim."
      alt: 'Masked mean-squared error for a tabular value fit to gamma=0.9 discounted returns decreases from 0.3258 initially to 0.1359 after 250 gradient updates on 512 sampled episodes.'
      code: "visual_data = {'kind':'line','x':list(range(len(loss_history))),'xlabel':'gradient updates','ylabel':'masked value prediction MSE','series':[{'label':'sampled rollout, seed 313','y':np.asarray(loss_history).tolist()}]}"
    runtime:
      kind: 'cpu'
    references:
      0:
        title: 'JAX: scan over a leading axis'
        url: 'https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/_autosummary/jax.lax.scan.html'
      1:
        title: 'JAX: value and gradient'
        url: 'https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/_autosummary/jax.value_and_grad.html'
      2:
        title: 'Schulman et al.: High-Dimensional Continuous Control Using Generalized Advantage Estimation'
        url: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.02438'
